1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:05,499
Nine-forty P.M. A sales laptop runs an unsigned installer.

2
00:00:05,799 --> 00:00:11,694
Within a minute, its saved passwords and session cookies are being read, and an archive leaves the network.

3
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,790
By morning, alerts are piling up across endpoint, DNS, firewall and sign-in logs.

4
00:00:18,090 --> 00:00:20,263
The hard part isn't receiving alerts.

5
00:00:20,563 --> 00:00:23,364
It's connecting them, before the incident escalates.

6
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:31,066
Caventas transforms fragmented evidence into an evolving, evidence-backed incident investigation.

7
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,103
Built for Tier 1 and junior Tier 2 analysts.

8
00:00:34,403 --> 00:00:35,619
The agent recommends.

9
00:00:35,919 --> 00:00:37,200
The analyst decides.

10
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:40,809
An analyst opens a case, and gets a case ID.

11
00:00:41,109 --> 00:00:47,383
Evidence goes in as it arrives: sign-ins, endpoint, DNS, firewall logs, and analyst notes.

12
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:52,423
Every record is hashed, tied to its source, and versioned into the incident state.

13
00:00:53,300 --> 00:01:00,512
Caventas rebuilds the timeline, and extracts the accounts, hosts, processes, files, domains and sessions involved.

14
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:06,353
Each change creates an immutable snapshot, whose relationships trace back to evidence.

15
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:08,960
Then the planning agent runs.

16
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:17,463
It loads one exact state version, analyses the evidence, retrieves only the policy rules for this stage, and proposes actions.

17
00:01:17,763 --> 00:01:23,697
The backend validates every tool, target, evidence reference and policy, before storing the plan.

18
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:27,263
The verdict: infostealer and session hijack.

19
00:01:27,563 --> 00:01:32,561
Each action explains why, cites its evidence, and names the policy that governs it.

20
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:35,258
Nothing runs without an analyst.

21
00:01:35,558 --> 00:01:38,853
The password reset is rejected, with the reason recorded.

22
00:01:39,153 --> 00:01:44,469
Host isolation is approved, and runs in a bounded simulator, never on live systems.

23
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:46,923
But executed isn't effective.

24
00:01:47,223 --> 00:01:52,977
Revoking sessions only partly works: the CRM's admin API is down for maintenance.

25
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:55,090
Then new evidence lands.

26
00:01:55,390 --> 00:02:02,648
The stolen CRM session is replayed from an unfamiliar device, and twelve hundred customer records are exported.

27
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:08,859
Approvals bound to the old state version are flagged stale, and the backend refuses to run them.

28
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:15,025
The agent re-plans against the new state: revoke the sessions again, and disable the account.

29
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:17,506
Policy decides who may approve.

30
00:02:17,806 --> 00:02:22,962
Disabling an account needs the IT manager, so a SOC analyst is refused.

31
00:02:23,262 --> 00:02:24,960
With the right role, it goes through.

32
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:28,232
And this time, revocation is validated effective.

33
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,103
Every agent proposal, and every human decision, stays on the record.

34
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:45,830
Finally, Caventas compiles a structured report from the stored records: classification, timeline, affected assets, ATT&CK techniques, decisions, validation results, and the gaps that remain.

35
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:48,069
Rendered to PDF.

36
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:53,087
From fragmented alerts, to an accountable response.

37
00:02:53,387 --> 00:02:57,678
Caventas helps analysts understand, decide, and act with confidence.

